<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dos Pilas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/629">629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dos Pilas is a Maya site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petexbatun">Petexbatún</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peten">Petén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, founded by an offshoot of the Tikal dynasty in 629 CE to control trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasion-river">Pasión River</a>. In 648 its ruler B'alaj Chan K'awiil, who claimed the Tikal throne, broke away and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a>; Tikal captured the city in 672, but Dos Pilas defeated Tikal in 679, conquered neighbouring cities and ruled with Aguateca as twin capitals of the Petexbatún kingdom. After Calakmul's defeat, Tamarindito and other centres rebelled, and in 761 the city was abandoned. Refugees threw up walls of stone stripped from its temples and palaces, but their village was overrun early in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dos-pilas.jpg" alt="Dos Pilas" width="330" height="518" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joanbanjo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panell_19,_Dos_Pilas,_museu_Nacional_d'Arqueologia_i_Etnologia,_Guatemala_(MUNAE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dos Pilas, Guatemalan Spanish for two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, lies between the Pasión and Salinas rivers in the municipality of Sayaxché, about 8 kilometres from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and 120 kilometres south-west of Tikal. The land is one of forested ridges, wetlands and lakes with heavy rainfall. The city was built in an area with little earlier settlement, 4 km from Arroyo de Piedra, the seat with Tamarindito of the kingdom that had dominated the region since the Early Classic. At its height the Dos Pilas kingdom covered some 3,885 square kilometres. It used the same emblem glyph as Tikal, Mutul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bʼalaj Chan Kʼawiil, installed as a child by his father Kʼinich Muwaan Jol II of Tikal, was captured by Yuknoom Chʼeen II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calakmul">Calakmul</a> and reinstated as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. After the victory over Tikal he made marriage alliances, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lady-six-sky">Lady Six Sky</a> was sent to refound the dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naranjo">Naranjo</a>. His son Itzamnaaj Kʼawiil, king from 698, oversaw a new victory over Tikal in 705, when its lord was captured by his general Uchaʼan Kʼin Bʼalam; tribute in labour and wealth then paid for the El Duende group, a hill terraced into the city's largest pyramid.</p><p>Uchaʼan Kʼin Bʼalam, enthroned in 727 probably as regent for the young heir Kʼawiil Chan Kʼinich, captured Yichʼaak Bʼalam, king of the much larger city of Seibal, in 735 and kept him as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. Kʼawiil Chan Kʼinich fought Ahkul and El Chorro in 743 and, two years later, the distant cities of Yaxchilán and Motul de San José. A hieroglyphic stairway at Tamarindito records his forced flight in 761, and the royal family probably moved to Aguateca. Itzan, Cancuén and Machaquila grew stronger as Dos Pilas fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins cover about one square kilometre around three complexes on an east-west axis, and are poorly preserved because their stone was stripped for the last defensive walls. Among them are the Main Group with its hieroglyphic stairways, the Bat Palace, where a smashed royal throne was found, and a tomb under Structure L5-1 thought to be Itzamnaaj Kʼawiil's. Twenty-two caves lie around the site. The ruins were first reported in 1953–4 by José and Lisandro Flores, were excavated by Arthur Demarest and Juan Antonio Valdés from 1989 to 1994, and were declared a National Monument on 12 June 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dos Pilas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Worse came when <b>Dos Pilas</b>, an outpost ruled by a Tikal prince, B'alaj Chan K'awiil, went over to Kaanul: in 657 Calakmul's king Yuknoom Ch'een II drove Nuun Ujol Chaak from Tikal, and in 679 Dos Pilas defeated Tikal in battle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tikal">Tikal · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The two greatest powers, Tikal and the Snake kingdom of Kaanul, ruled from Dzibanche and later from Calakmul, contended for supremacy through much of the 6th to 8th centuries, drawing in allies from Caracol to <b>Dos Pilas</b> and Naranjo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The most powerful took the title kaloomte' and ruled other kings as vassals: in the 7th century Yuknoom Ch’een II of Calakmul controlled the kings of <b>Dos Pilas</b>, Cancuén and Naranjo.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li></ul></section>
